    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">Reading behaviour inside the home</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">In parent counselling the focus is not the child alone but the relationship between child and parent. A child's behaviour takes on its meaning alongside everything else that happens at home.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">Think of a child who won't go to school in the mornings. Treat it only as the child's anxiety and you miss how those mornings go at home: who raises their voice, who steps in, how the argument stops the moment the child starts getting ready. School refusal sometimes works to hold the tension in the house in one place.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-8">This view blames no one. Most parents are already doing what they can; the difficulty usually lies not in the intention but in a method that does not land with this particular child. Once &ldquo;what am I doing wrong&rdquo; gives way to &ldquo;how does this loop keep turning&rdquo;, what could change at home becomes visible.</p>
        <div class="bg-primary/5 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-primary/15">
          <blockquote class="font-serif text-xl text-ink leading-relaxed italic">
            "The youngest member's difficulty can sometimes be the voice of a loop running through the home."
          </blockquote>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-warm">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-8">How the sessions run</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="flex gap-5">
            <div class="flex-shrink-0"><div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-2xl bg-primary text-white flex items-center justify-center font-semibold text-lg">1</div></div>
            <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary flex-1">
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">We get to know you and your child</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">What started when, what has been tried at home, what helped and what made things harder. Both parents can come, or one on their own. The first session is for assessment; you will not be handed a list of actions on the way out.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex gap-5">
            <div class="flex-shrink-0"><div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-2xl bg-primary text-white flex items-center justify-center font-semibold text-lg">2</div></div>
            <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary flex-1">
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">We make the repeating loop visible</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">We look at the moment just before the behaviour and the moment after: which sentence starts the argument, who withdraws, who steps in, how it closes. Once that sequence is visible, what could be done differently at home shows up too.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex gap-5">
            <div class="flex-shrink-0"><div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-2xl bg-primary text-white flex items-center justify-center font-semibold text-lg">3</div></div>
            <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary flex-1">
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">We build an approach you can sustain</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">We look not for the correct method from a book but for the one that works in your house. You try it between sessions and then we look at it together; the part that did not work shows which assumption needs to change. Sessions last 50 minutes and usually take place weekly or every two weeks.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-8">Two things parents often ask</h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-serif text-lg text-ink mb-3">&ldquo;My partner doesn't want to come&rdquo;</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">This happens often. The reluctance usually comes from a worry about being blamed, or a belief that it will turn out to be pointless. We don't try to talk the other parent into attending. Starting with the parent who does come already changes something in the loop at home, and the other parent sometimes joins in later sessions.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-serif text-lg text-ink mb-3">&ldquo;Is what's said in a separate session passed on?&rdquo;</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Sometimes we see one parent, or the child, on their own. How anything raised there will be handled is agreed in advance. Sessions and your personal information are protected in line with our confidentiality principles.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-warm">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">When generations live side by side</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">In Cyprus families often live close to one another. Grandparents are part of daily care, relatives live on the same street, and decisions about a child are rarely made without consulting the older generation. That closeness carries a family; it is also where boundaries blur.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">Young parents building their own household carry the expectations of two families at once. A child meets one rule at home and another at their grandmother's. The conflict usually comes not from ill will but from two versions of what is right, both trying to hold at the same time.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The aim in these sessions is not to move anyone away from their family. We look together at where a boundary can be set without breaking the relationship, who has a say in which decision about the child, and how expectations can be spoken about.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">Parent counselling, or another route?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">If the difficulty circulates in the approach at home and the parent-child relationship, parent counselling is a reasonable place to start. Where a child needs their own space for their emotional world, <a href="/en/services/child-psychology.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">child psychology</a> or <a href="/en/services/adolescent-psychology.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">adolescent psychology</a> sessions can run alongside the work with you, depending on age.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">Parent counselling is not therapy for the parent: the focus is not your own process but the relationship you have with your child. If you find your own process needs its own space, <a href="/en/services/individual-therapy.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">individual therapy</a> is a separate frame for that.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">For parents who live in different cities or countries, <a href="/en/services/online-therapy.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">online sessions</a> are an option. For more on how the process runs, see <a href="/en/blog/parent-counselling.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">what parent counselling is</a> and <a href="/en/blog/family-transitions-parent-counselling.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">parent counselling through difficult family periods</a>. We consider which route suits your situation together in the first session.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
